(FUN-40 — printing a call result DIRECTLY, without routing it through a
 declared variable, is correct for every type as of 0.4.10. On 0.4.9 and
 earlier it was correct for text, boolean and list but rendered a `float`
 as its raw IEEE-754 bit pattern — even though the return type WAS
 declared, which is the thing vox bug #45 says fixes this class (2.5 as a
 double is 0x4004000000000000 = 4612811918334230528). A `map` return was
 broken the same way, printing a raw heap address; it is left out of THIS
 probe only because the address varies per run and would make the probe
 uncomparable — D5.vox carries that half. See Discrepancy 5.
 RESOLVED, 2026-08-22 — vox #67, same fix as D5.vox. Re-run verbatim
 against 0.4.10: the direct-print `float` now agrees with the
 routed-through-a-variable print.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox FUN-40.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   2.5
   2.5
   seven
   1
   [1, 2, 3]
)
To 'give float'. Return a float, 2.5.
To 'give text'. Return a text, "seven".
To 'give boolean'. Return a boolean, true.
To 'give list'. Return a list, [1, 2, 3].

a float called routed is 'give float'.
Print routed.
Print 'give float'.
Print 'give text'.
Print 'give boolean'.
Print 'give list'.
